[%think 3617] [%out 3978] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2674] The black
rook on d5 attacks my cannon on d1. Nothing protects that cannon. The plan is
to jump my c2 knight to d4. There it blocks the rook and also serves as a
screen, so my d1 cannon can hit the rook on d5. - If the black b5 knight takes
on d4, my cannon can take the rook on d5. - If the black rook takes on d4, my
c6 knight recaptures. Black's b5 knight can take back again. That trades my two
knights for its rook, and the material balance stays about the same. - One
cost: the a3 pawn loses its protector, and the b5 knight can take it. 便条:
Played c2d4. The knight blocks the d-file and screens for the d1 cannon to hit
the d5 rook. If b5 takes d4, the cannon takes the rook on d5. If the rook takes
d4, recapture with the c6 knight; black's b5 knight can take back, trading my
two knights for its rook. The a3 pawn now has no protector. Don't capture on e7
with the knight. I'm still down material, so play solid and look for chances. }
